        ("What does the central limit theorem justify here?", "Averages of many weakly dependent transactions get closer to normal, which is why a z-test on a large fraud-rate difference is often fine. It does not make one customer's spending normal.", "Invoking the CLT for a sample of 8 defaults."),
        ("How do you read a 95% confidence interval for a default rate?", "If the sampling model is right and I repeated the study, about 95% of such intervals would cover the true rate. This interval is not a 95% probability statement about the true rate unless I am being explicitly Bayesian.", "Saying there is a 95% chance the true rate lies in the reported interval, in a frequentist sentence."),
        ("What is a p-value?", "The probability, if the null is true, of a result at least as extreme as the one observed. It is not the probability the null is true and not the size of the effect.", "Ranking models by p-value."),
