# Further statistics items, still specific.
_MORE_STATS = [
    ("Define likelihood versus probability in one sentence.", "Probability is of data given a fixed parameter. Likelihood is that same expression read as a function of the parameter given the data.", "Maximizing a likelihood and calling the maximum a probability of the parameter."),
    ("What is a standard error?", "The standard deviation of a sampling distribution, such as the distribution of a mean. It shrinks as the sample grows. The standard deviation of the data does not.", "Calling the sample standard deviation a standard error."),
    ("Why does the margin of error shrink with the square root of n?", "Independent noise averages down. Quadrupling a simple random sample halves the standard error of the mean, under the usual assumptions.", "Expecting a doubled sample to cut the interval in half."),
    ("What is a sampling frame, and why does it matter for a credit model?", "It is the list you actually draw from. If declined applicants are missing, the model describes approved customers, not applicants.", "Training on funded loans and applying the score to all applicants without saying the populations differ."),
    ("What is selection bias?", "The sample is distorted by how people entered it. Approved customers, survived products, and investigated alerts are all selected samples.", "Fitting a default model only on approved loans and ignoring the applicants you never saw."),
    ("What is measurement bias in a fraud label?", "The label is confirmed fraud, which depends on what was reviewed. Unreviewed fraud is labeled good. The model learns the review process as well as the fraud.", "Treating is_fraud as a perfect observation of all fraud."),
    ("What is survivorship bias in a scorecard?", "Building the development sample from accounts that still exist drops the ones that charged off and left, or the products that were shut down.", "A dataset of currently open accounts presented as the full origination history."),
    ("What is an unbiased estimator?", "Its expectation equals the parameter. Unbiased does not mean low variance, and a biased estimator can have lower error.", "Rejecting every biased estimator, including many regularized models, on principle."),
    ("Why is sample variance divided by n-1?", "Dividing by n underestimates the population variance on average when the mean is estimated from the same sample. n-1 corrects that for the simple iid case.", "Using n-1 in a z-score and n in the training pipeline without noticing."),
    ("What is covariance?", "The expected product of deviations from the two means. Positive covariance means the variables tend to be high together.", "Reading a covariance of 10 as a stronger relationship than a covariance of 0.2 without looking at units."),
    ("How do you interpret a correlation of 0.1 in a huge transaction sample?", "The linear association is weak. The p-value can still be tiny because n is large. Weak and detectable is still weak.", "Calling it a strong relationship because it is significant."),
    ("What is the law of large numbers, in practice?", "The sample average settles near the expected value as independent observations accumulate. A single customer's average does not settle just because the book is large.", "Averaging one customer's five transactions and calling it a stable mean."),
    ("When is the normal approximation to a proportion poor?", "When np and n(1-p) are small. A fraud rate of 0.1% needs a large n before the symmetric interval is honest. Otherwise use a method built for rare events.", "A Wald interval on 2 frauds out of 200 transactions."),
    ("What is a hypothesis in a model-monitoring test?", "A statement you can refute, such as 'the fraud rate is unchanged' or 'the score distribution matches training'. The alternative should be the change you would act on.", "A hypothesis of 'the model got worse' with no definition of worse."),
    ("Why is a one-sided test sometimes the wrong default?", "It hides a change in the other direction. A fraud rate that falls can be a labeling outage. Two-sided is the honest default unless the action is truly one-directional and pre-stated.", "Using a one-sided test after seeing the direction."),
    ("What is a t-test doing that a z-test is not?", "It uses an estimated standard deviation and a heavier tail, which matters in small samples. With thousands of transactions the two are close.", "Insisting on a t-test for a proportion."),
    ("What is a paired comparison?", "Two measurements on the same unit, such as the old and new score on the same application. Pairing removes the unit's baseline and usually increases power.", "An unpaired test of scores when every application was scored twice."),
    ("What is a chi-square test useful for here?", "A difference in the distribution of a categorical variable, such as merchant category mix between two months. It says the mix changed. It does not say why.", "A significant chi-square treated as proof of a fraud-pattern change."),
    ("What is an odds ratio?", "The odds of the event in one group divided by the odds in another. A logistic coefficient is a log odds ratio for a one-unit change, holding the other modeled features fixed.", "Reading a logistic coefficient as a change in probability."),
    ("Why can a logistic coefficient and a probability move differently?", "The logistic curve is flatter in the tails. The same log-odds change is a small probability change near 0 or 1 and a larger one near 0.5.", "Telling a stakeholder that a coefficient of 0.2 is a 20% default-rate increase."),
    ("What is calibration?", "Among applications scored 0.1, about 10% should default if the label window matches the training definition. Calibration is about the probability, not the ranking.", "A high AUC offered as proof the probabilities can be used as PD."),
    ("What is the Brier score?", "The mean squared error of the predicted probabilities. It punishes both poor ranking and poor calibration. It is not comparable across wildly different base rates without care.", "Comparing Brier scores from a 0.1% fraud book and a 10% default book as if they were the same scale."),
    ("What is overfitting, in a validation sentence?", "The model fits training noise, so training error is optimistic relative to a later window. A time-based gap between the two numbers is the symptom I trust.", "A small train-test gap on a random split of a time series, offered as evidence against overfitting."),
    ("What is regularization doing?", "It penalizes complexity so the fit moves less toward noise. It is not a substitute for a clean target and a clean split.", "Adding L2 and skipping the leakage review."),
    ("What is bias and variance of a model, not of a sample?", "Bias is error from a too-simple pattern. Variance is error from a fit that jumps when the training sample changes. The split and the noise set the tradeoff.", "Using bias to mean unfairness in the same sentence without saying which one."),
    ("What is a confidence interval not?", "It is not a prediction interval for the next customer, and it is not a promise that the next month's rate will fall inside it if the process changes.", "Using a confidence interval for the mean loss as a risk limit for one new loan."),
    ("What is a prediction interval?", "A range for a new observation, wider than the interval for the mean, because it includes the noise of that one draw.", "Quoting the confidence interval of the mean forecast as if it covered the next day's return."),
    ("How do you explain r-squared to a non-technical partner?", "It is the share of variance in the training target the model lines up with. It is not the probability the model is right, and it can be high in-sample and useless out of time.", "A high r-squared as the business case for a pricing change."),
    ("What is multicollinearity doing to a credit scorecard?", "Correlated features split one story across coefficients, which bounce and look unstable. The ranking can still be fine. Do not over-interpret a single coefficient.", "Dropping a feature only because its sign looked odd, without checking its pair."),
    ("What is a residual?", "Actual minus fitted. A pattern in residuals against time or against a segment is a missing feature or a broken assumption, not a reason to quote the average residual, which is often near zero by construction.", "Stopping the analysis because the mean residual is zero."),
    ("What is heteroscedasticity?", "The noise scale changes with the level or the segment. A single standard error then misstates uncertainty for the expensive segment.", "One interval for every customer when ticket sizes differ by orders of magnitude."),
    ("What is a robust statistic?", "A summary that does not jump when a few points are extreme. The median and a trimmed mean are robust. The mean of raw transaction amounts often is not.", "A mean ticket size dominated by three wires, reported as typical."),
    ("When is the median a better summary than the mean?", "When the distribution is skewed and the question is 'what is typical', not 'what is the total'. Loss and exposure still want sums and means.", "Replacing expected loss with a median loss."),
    ("What is a quantile?", "The value that a chosen share of the sample falls at or below. A 95th percentile amount is a tail summary, not a z-score.", "Calling the 95th percentile a 5% probability of fraud."),
    ("What is a kernel density estimate useful for?", "A smooth picture of a distribution. It is a visual, not a test. The bandwidth can hide or invent a second mode.", "A bimodal density treated as two customer types without a table of counts."),
    ("What is the difference between a parameter and a statistic?", "A parameter is a property of the process. A statistic is computed from the sample. The sample default rate estimates the process default rate and is not identical to it.", "Talking about the true AUC of this particular test set as if the test set were the process."),
    ("What is an iid assumption?", "Independent and identically distributed. Transaction rows usually violate both: the same customer repeats, and the mix changes. Methods that assume iid need a caveat or a clustered design.", "A random split described as creating iid rows."),
    ("How do you sample when customers have many transactions?", "Sample customers, then take their transactions, if the decision is about customers. Sampling rows over-represents heavy users.", "A transaction-level random sample presented as a customer study."),
    ("What is a stratified sample?", "You draw inside segments so a rare segment is present. The analysis then needs weights if you want a number that represents the whole book.", "An unweighted metric on a sample that oversampled the risky segment."),
    ("What is a population in this lab?", "The process you want the next decision to face, not the CSV you happened to generate. A synthetic generator is a known process, which is why it is useful for teaching and not a substitute for a real book.", "Calling the training file the population."),
    ("What is a likelihood ratio, informally?", "How many times more likely the data are under one explanation than another. It is a comparison of explanations, not a probability of either explanation by itself.", "A likelihood ratio of 10 described as a 10% chance."),
    ("What is shrinkage?", "Pulling an estimate toward a common value because the group is small. A merchant with two transactions should not get a raw fraud rate of 50% as if it were precise.", "A raw rate for a tiny segment deployed as a feature."),
    ("What is a prior in a production conversation?", "What you already believe about the rate before this sample, ideally from history. It is not a way to force the answer you want.", "A prior chosen after seeing the result."),
    ("What is posterior predictive checking?", "Use the fitted model to simulate data and see whether the simulations look like the real patterns you care about, such as the tail of amounts. If they do not, the model is missing something even if a p-value looks fine.", "Stopping at an in-sample loss."),
    ("What is exchangeability?", "The unit labels could be permuted without changing your beliefs. Customers in a new country may not be exchangeable with the development sample.", "Pooling a new market into an old model because the schema matches."),
    ("How do you talk about uncertainty to a risk committee?", "Give the range of loss or approval that is consistent with the sample, say what would push you outside it, and do not hide the range behind a single PD.", "One PD to two decimals and no interval, no vintage, and no caveat."),
    ("What is a false discovery rate?", "Among the slices you called significant, the share you expect to be false. It is the right family of ideas when you scan many merchant categories.", "Bonferroni or nothing, with no statement of how many cuts you looked at."),
    ("Why is a single holdout not the same as a confidence interval?", "A holdout is one future-like sample. It gives a point estimate of performance. Variability across origins or bootstraps of the holdout is what makes an interval.", "One test AUC reported as if it had no uncertainty."),
    ("What is Simpson's paradox in approval rates?", "A segment can show a higher approval rate inside every score band and a lower rate overall, because the segment sits in worse bands. The mix is the story.", "A single approval-rate gap quoted without the score mix."),
    ("How should you compare two models on the same customers?", "Use a paired comparison of the losses or the ranks on the same out-of-time rows. Two separate averages throw away the pairing.", "Two AUC numbers from different windows compared as if they were the same test."),
    ("What is a minimum detectable effect?", "The smallest change your sample can reliably catch at the power and significance you chose. If it is larger than the change you would act on, the test cannot help you.", "A completed test with no power calculation and a claim of no difference."),
    ("What is leakage in a statistical test of a model?", "Using the test window to pick the feature, the cutoff, and the metric, then quoting that same window as confirmation. The test is no longer a fresh sample.", "A holdout that was used five times and is still called untouched."),
    ("Why can a precise estimate be the wrong estimand?", "You can estimate the default rate of approved loans very precisely and still not have the default rate of applicants. Precision does not fix the wrong target.", "A tight interval around a selected population, presented as the book."),
    ("What is a sensitivity analysis?", "Recompute the decision under other reasonable assumptions: different LGD, different costs, a later window. If the recommendation flips, say so.", "One base case and a recommendation that hides the flip."),
    ("How do you check that a random number generator experiment is stable?", "Rerun with a second seed, or a small seed sweep, and see whether the decision changes. One seed is a reproducible anecdote.", "A single synthetic run treated as the population answer."),
    ("What is attenuation from noisy features?", "A feature measured with error usually looks less related to the target than the true feature is. A weak coefficient can be a dirty field.", "Dropping a weakly measured but important field only because the coefficient is small."),
    ("What is a confounding variable in a credit story?", "A variable that influences both the feature you want to talk about and the default. Income, age of account, and product can confound a raw utilization comparison.", "A univariate default rate by utilization presented as the effect of utilization."),
    ("What does 'controlling for' mean in a regression sentence?", "The coefficient is the association given the other features in the model. It is not a controlled experiment unless the design was an experiment.", "Language of causality from an observational scorecard."),
    ("What is a goodness-of-fit test not telling you?", "That the model is useful. A model can fit the training distribution and fail the decision, or fail a formal test and still rank well enough to use with caution.", "A rejected goodness-of-fit test as an automatic kill, or a pass as an automatic ship."),
    ("How do you describe a small sample of defaults?", "Say the count. Twenty defaults cannot support a large feature list. Prefer a simple model, a wide interval, and a plan to revisit when the vintage seasons.", "A ten-feature model on fifteen defaults with a quoted AUC."),
    ("What is the interpretation of a KS statistic?", "The largest gap between the score distributions of the two classes. It is a ranking summary. It does not pick a cutoff and it ignores costs.", "Choosing the score at the KS point as the business threshold without a cost."),
    ("Why might a significant fraud-rate change still be left alone?", "Because the dollar impact is inside the noise of the loss budget, or because the cause is a known product launch you already priced. Significance is not a runbook.", "An automatic retrain on every significant rate move."),
    ("What is an instrumental interpretation you should not overclaim?", "A model feature is not an instrument. You usually cannot say what would happen if you intervened on it. You can say it ranks the next similar application.", "Policy language that sounds like a causal effect from a predictive model."),
    ("How do you validate a confidence interval in a simulator?", "Generate many samples from a known rate, compute the interval each time, and check the coverage. That is a teaching check this synthetic lab can actually do.", "Trusting the formula on clustered data because the coverage worked on iid draws."),
    ("What is a standard normal critical value doing in a 95% interval?", "About 1.96 multiplies the standard error. It comes from the normal sampling model. If that model is wrong, the 1.96 does not create 95% coverage.", "Memorizing 1.96 and skipping the sampling story."),
    ("Why report both relative and absolute risk?", "A rate that doubles from 0.1% to 0.2% is a 100% relative increase and a 0.1 point absolute increase. Loss cares about the absolute counts.", "A relative increase with no baseline rate."),
    ("What is a winsorized mean?", "A mean computed after clipping the extremes to chosen quantiles. It reduces the pull of bad amount fields. Say the cut, because it changes the number.", "Winsorizing and still calling the result the expected loss of the raw book."),
    ("What is a trimmed estimator hiding?", "The tail you trimmed may be the loss you care about. Trimming for a typical ticket is fine. Trimming before an expected-loss sum is not.", "A trimmed mean used as portfolio loss."),
    ("How do you explain interaction to a stakeholder?", "The association of one feature depends on another. Night spending may matter only for a new device. A single coefficient cannot say that unless the interaction is in the model or you show the slice.", "One global night effect when the table shows it is confined to one segment."),
    ("What is a partial dependence plot, carefully described?", "The model's average prediction as you vary one feature in the data you have. It is not a causal response curve.", "A partial dependence curve used as a pricing elasticity."),
    ("What is the ecological fallacy?", "A relationship at the country or segment level need not hold for the customer. A high-fraud country can contain mostly ordinary customers.", "Targeting every customer in a high-rate segment as if they shared the segment rate."),
    ("What is regression to the mean in a fraud queue?", "Accounts picked for an extreme week tend to look calmer next week even if you do nothing. A before-after chart without a comparison group will flatter the intervention.", "Crediting a rule for a drop that followed an extreme week."),
    ("How do you set a sample size in words?", "Choose the smallest difference that would change the decision, choose the error rates you can live with, and the required n follows from the base rate and the variance. If you cannot get that n, say the test will be inconclusive.", "Collecting 'as much as possible' and testing anyway with no comment on power."),
    ("What is a clustered standard error trying to fix?", "Dependence inside a customer or a merchant. It widens the interval. It does not fix a biased sample.", "A clustered error presented as a solution to selection bias."),
    ("What is the difference between a fixed cutoff and a top-k review list?", "A cutoff flags every score above a line, so volume moves when the score distribution moves. Top-k flags a fixed capacity. Operations often live in top-k even when the model discussion is about a cutoff.", "A threshold chosen offline that the queue cannot hold when scores drift."),
    ("Why is a calibration slope useful?", "A slope below one means probabilities are too extreme. Ranking can remain useful while the PD you multiply by LGD is not.", "Feeding a miscalibrated score straight into expected loss."),
    ("What is an empirical Bayes idea in merchant rates?", "Shrink a merchant's fraud rate toward the global rate in proportion to how few events it has. The shrunk rate is a better feature than the raw rate for rare merchants.", "A raw 1-out-of-1 fraud rate as a merchant feature."),
    ("How do you state a result without overclaiming?", "Give the estimate, the window, the comparison, and the decision it supports. Leave out causal verbs you did not earn.", "The model reduced fraud, when the evidence is a higher AUC on a test file."),
    ("What is a pre-registration analogue for a model review?", "Write the split, the metric, and the decision rule before you look at the test window. It is how you keep the test window honest.", "Changing the metric after the test numbers are known."),
    ("What does a non-parametric method buy you?", "Fewer assumptions about the shape. Usually wider uncertainty or a larger sample requirement. It is not automatically more correct.", "A non-parametric test as a way to avoid thinking about dependence."),
    ("What is a hazard, in a default setting, in one sentence?", "The chance of defaulting in the next small window, given survival so far. A 12-month PD is not the same object as a monthly hazard, though they are related.", "Using a monthly hazard and a 12-month PD interchangeably."),
    ("Why can censoring bias a default rate?", "Loans that have not reached 12 months have not had the full chance to default. Counting them as good understates PD.", "A default rate that includes this month's originations in the denominator as if they were seasoned."),
    ("What is a competing risk, informally?", "The loan can close for another reason, such as prepayment, before it defaults. Ignoring that path misstates the default probability you think you are estimating.", "A PD model that treats prepaid loans as if they were simply non-defaults with no comment."),
    ("How do you use a negative control in a data study?", "A comparison that should not move if your story is right. If it moves, the story is confounded. It is a habit, not a proof.", "One supportive slice and no slice that could have embarrassed the story."),
    ("What is the prosecutor's fallacy?", "Confusing P(evidence|innocent) with P(innocent|evidence). In fraud, a rare device pattern is not automatically a guilty transaction.", "A rare feature combination treated as near-certain fraud."),
    ("What is a proper scoring rule?", "A score that is optimized, in expectation, by reporting your true probabilities. Brier score and log loss are proper. Accuracy is not.", "Tuning a probability model on accuracy."),
    ("Why might log loss and AUC disagree?", "Log loss cares about the probabilities. AUC cares about order. A miscalibrated but well-ordered model can win AUC and lose log loss.", "Picking the winner with a metric that does not match the decision."),
    ("What is a winsorized fraud amount hiding?", "The large losses. A typical fraud ticket and the loss tail are different reports. Show both.", "One average fraud amount as the cost of a miss."),
    ("How do you explain a wide interval?", "The sample does not pin the number down. The right action is often to collect the vintage or to pool carefully, not to pretend the midpoint is exact.", "Filling the width with a more complicated model."),
    ("What is data snooping?", "Searching many specifications and quoting the best as if it were the only one you tried. The test window and the metric have to be protected from that search.", "Twenty feature sets and the best test AUC."),
    ("What is the difference between a training metric and a decision metric?", "Training can use log loss or a weighted loss to fit. The decision uses cost, capacity, or a seasoned default rate. They should be related and they are not the same number.", "Deploying the model with the best training loss and no operating point."),
    ("How do you check a random split was not used on a panel?", "Look at the maximum training timestamp and the minimum test timestamp, and look for the same customer on both sides if the feature uses that customer's future.", "A sklearn train_test_split with shuffle=True on a sorted extract, assumed safe because the function is standard."),
    ("What is an influence point?", "A row that moves the fit a lot. A single huge wire can own a regression of amount on features. Inspect it before you interpret the slope.", "A coefficient story that disappears when one row is removed, told without that check."),
    ("What does 'statistically significant but small' imply for a rollout?", "You can see the change, and the change may not be worth the operational cost. Do the cost arithmetic.", "A rollout justified only by the star on the p-value."),
    ("What is a two-sample problem versus a two-period problem?", "Two independent groups are a two-sample test. The same book in two months is a time comparison with dependence and mix shift. The second needs the mix check.", "A two-proportion test on two months with no mix table."),
    ("How would you teach a new hire to read a lift chart?", "As you move down the score, show the cumulative share of defaults captured versus the share of applications touched. The value is the extra defaults in the slice you can afford to treat.", "A lift chart with no base rate and no capacity line."),
    ("What is a confusion matrix missing?", "Costs and the base rate's business meaning. Four counts become a decision only after you price the off-diagonals and say which grain they are.", "A confusion matrix at 0.5 as the final slide."),
    ("Why is a single threshold accuracy number not comparable across months?", "If the base rate moves, accuracy moves even when the ranking is unchanged. Compare rates, precision at a fixed review volume, or a cost.", "Month-over-month accuracy as model health."),
    ("What is a standardized difference?", "A mean gap divided by a scale, so segments are comparable. It is a description. It is not a p-value and not a legal conclusion.", "A standardized difference labeled as discrimination."),
    ("How do you report a proportion near zero?", "Report the count and the denominator. A rate of 0.000 with a denominator of 40 is not a precise zero. An interval or a statement of the count prevents false comfort.", "0.00% with no count, on a small segment."),
    ("What is the role of a seed in a teaching lab?", "It makes a synthetic example repeatable so a metric can be discussed. It does not make the example a forecast of a real portfolio.", "A seeded demo presented as a production backtest."),
]
TOPIC_QUESTIONS += _bulk("statistics", _MORE_STATS)

TOPIC_QUESTIONS += _bulk(
    "machine_learning",
    [
        ("What is the difference between a ranking metric and a decision?", "AUC and KS describe order. A threshold, a review capacity, or an expected-loss cutoff is the decision. I fit for ranking or likelihood and I choose the operating point on a later window.", "Shipping the threshold that maximized accuracy on the training fold."),
        ("When is logistic regression enough?", "When the signal is roughly linear in well-built features, you need a coefficient story, and a tree does not beat it by enough to matter. It is a strong credit and fraud baseline.", "Skipping it because boosting is more modern."),
        ("When is a gradient-boosted tree worth it?", "When interactions and non-linearities are real and a proper out-of-time test shows a gain in the decision metric, not only in training loss.", "A leaderboard win on a random split."),
        ("What is class_weight doing?", "It increases the loss on the rare class so the fit does not ignore it. The resulting predict_proba is not a population probability until you calibrate it.", "Reading a class-weighted probability as a PD."),
        ("Why calibrate on the training procedure and not on the test set?", "Calibration is part of the model. Using the test set to fit it makes the test score optimistic. I calibrate with cross-fitting inside training or on the validation window, then I freeze it.", "Isotonic regression fit on the test window."),
        ("What is a baseline you would be embarrassed to omit?", "A rule a risk analyst already believes: score below 620, or amount z-score above 3, or a velocity cap. If the model cannot beat it on the decision metric, I do not replace it.", "A comparison only against a coin flip."),
        ("How do you pick features without leaking?", "Every candidate must be computable at the decision from data dated at or before that decision. I drop identifiers that merely memorize an entity, and I test a point-in-time rebuild.", "Customer-level averages computed on the full file, then split."),
        ("What is training-serving skew?", "The feature in production is not the feature in training. A different null fill, a different window, or a fresher aggregate will move scores even when the weights are unchanged.", "Retraining the weights and not diffing the feature code."),
        ("How many features are too many?", "More than the rare class can support. A hundred fraud labels cannot identify hundreds of free parameters. Prefer a small set you can explain and monitor.", "A wide feature store dump into the model because the columns exist."),
        ("What is a hyperparameter search allowed to see?", "Training folds, or the training period. Not the final test period. I would rather under-tune than reuse the test window.", "GridSearchCV on the full dataset."),
        ("Why might accuracy rise while the business result gets worse?", "The majority class dominates accuracy. You can get more majority calls right and miss more of the expensive minority.", "An accuracy gate in a fraud CI pipeline."),
        ("What is a calibration curve?", "Predicted probability on the x-axis, observed frequency on the y-axis. A useful PD lies near the diagonal in the region where you actually approve or review.", "A curve fit on training data and shown as validation."),
        ("How do you handle a new merchant category?", "An explicit unknown level that the encoder already has, plus monitoring of how often it appears. I do not silently one-hot a new level into a new column the model lacks.", "Failing the scorer, or worse, aligning columns by position after the schema drifted."),
        ("What is early stopping using?", "A validation loss that is not the test set. It stops adding trees when that validation loss stalls. The test set stays unused.", "Early stopping on the test fold."),
        ("When would you refuse to add a feature you are allowed to use?", "When it proxies a protected attribute in a way you cannot justify, when it will not be available at decision time, or when it is a near-copy of the label.", "Using it because the AUC moved."),
    ],
)
TOPIC_QUESTIONS += _bulk(
    "predictive_modeling",
    [
        ("What is the unit of a prediction?", "The row the decision is about: a transaction, an application, a customer-month. Features, labels, and metrics have to share that grain.", "A customer label joined to transaction rows and an AUC quoted as if the rows were independent decisions."),
        ("What is a prediction horizon?", "The future window the label covers, such as default within 12 months or fraud confirmed later. The horizon defines when you are even allowed to score the outcome.", "A model whose label window is shorter than the business's loss window."),
        ("What is a baseline model in credit?", "A small logistic scorecard, or even a single score cut, evaluated on the same later originations as the challenger.", "A baseline fit on a different sample than the challenger."),
        ("How do you define the target so operations recognize it?", "In their language and their timing: charge-off at 120 days, or fraud confirmed by an investigator, not a convenient column that happens to be numeric.", "A proxy label nobody in risk would accept, with a strong AUC."),
        ("What errors are expensive?", "It depends on the decision. A missed default and a rejected good applicant are different costs. I write them down before I look at AUC.", "A symmetric loss for an asymmetric decision."),
        ("How do you stop a model from memorizing customers?", "Leave the raw id out, split by time or by customer, and do not give the model a feature that is unique per entity.", "Target encoding of customer id."),
        ("What is a champion-challenger setup?", "The current rule or model stays in the decision. The new model scores in shadow. You compare yield on the same decisions before you switch.", "Replacing the champion because the offline metric won."),
        ("How often should you retrain?", "When the feature mix or the seasoned outcome moves the decision, or on a schedule matched to how fast the book changes. Not every night by default.", "A nightly retrain on unseasoned labels."),
        ("What is a useful error analysis?", "Look at the false negatives and false positives by segment, time, and a few features. The global metric will not show a broken slice.", "A single AUC and no slice."),
        ("How do you explain a model you do not fully trust?", "Say what it ranks, on what window, against which baseline, and what it is not allowed to decide alone. Trust is a scope, not a vibe.", "A production recommendation from an offline notebook."),
        ("What is target encoding leakage?", "Replacing a category by the mean of the target computed with the row itself, or with the full sample before the split. The encoding has seen the answer.", "A target mean fit on all rows and merged back."),
        ("Why keep a simple model in production even after a complex one wins?", "It is the fallback when features are late, and it is the explanation when the complex model does something the business will not accept.", "Deleting the baseline the day the booster ships."),
        ("What is a data contract for a model?", "The columns, types, windows, and null rules the score depends on. A broken contract should fail or fall back, not score nonsense.", "Best-effort scoring when a required feature is missing."),
        ("How do you compare two models with different score scales?", "On a shared decision: precision at the same review volume, or cost at the threshold each model would actually use. Raw score differences are meaningless.", "Subtracting a logistic score from a tree score."),
        ("What does a learning curve tell you?", "Whether more data is still helping, on a split that matches deployment. A flat curve says more of the same rows will not fix a bad label or a leak.", "A learning curve on shuffled data as a reason to buy a bigger table."),
    ],
)
