        ("Why is a backtest not a cross-validation score?", "Cross-validation with shuffling mixes time. A backtest keeps the order and the information set of each origin.", "A TimeSeriesSplit reported as a trading backtest with no costs."),
        ("What is an expanding window?", "Train from the start through the origin, test the next block, then move the origin forward. It uses more history each time.", "An expanding window that also expands into the test block."),
        ("What is a rolling window?", "A fixed-length train set that ends at the origin. It forgets old regimes, which is a feature if those regimes are misleading and a bug if the sample gets too short.", "A rolling window shorter than the seasonality."),
        ("What is purge and embargo?", "Drop training labels whose horizon overlaps the test period, and optionally leave a gap. Overlapping daily labels otherwise leak.", "Purging the test set instead of the overlapping training labels."),
        ("How do transaction costs enter?", "As a drag proportional to turnover, in basis points, subtracted from the gross result. The parameter should be severe enough that a tiny edge does not survive by optimism.", "A cost of zero called conservative."),
        ("What is slippage?", "The fill is worse than the printed price. Buys are higher, sells are lower. It is an assumption, and I would show the result at more than one assumption.", "A mid price fill on a thin name."),
        ("What metrics belong in the report?", "Net result, drawdown, turnover, and the number of origins. A single Sharpe on the best variant is not a report.", "The maximum Sharpe across twenty unreported trials."),
        ("How do you avoid backtest overfitting?", "Pre-commit the rule, limit the variants, and keep a final period untouched. Report the distribution of origins.", "A parameter scan and the best cell."),
